(Plan 310 task 3 - the corners of value semantics that a two-line example
 never reaches, each of which fails silently rather than loudly if the
 copies are wrong.

 A call writes its result into storage the CALLER owns, so two calls to the
 same function in one argument list must not land on each other's bytes; a
 recursive call's parameter and result must live in its own frame; a thing
 argument past the six argument registers must be read off the stack like
 any other; a thing longer than the unrolled moves is copied by the same
 rule; and a global thing must be copyable into a function's frame and back
 out again.

 Acceptance: the eleven lines in the .expected, in that order.)

A thing called point has
  a number called x is 0,
  a number called y is 0.

A thing called segment has
  a point called start,
  a point called end.

A thing called journey has
  a point called home,
  a point called 'the station',
  a point called 'the office',
  a point called 'the shop',
  a point called 'the park'.

To 'stepped east' with a point called start.
  Set start's x to start's x add 1.
  Return a point, start.

To 'the segment between' with a point called 'one end' and a point called 'the other end'.
  a segment called 'the line'.
  Set 'the line''s start to 'one end'.
  Set 'the line''s end to 'the other end'.
  Return a segment, 'the line'.

To 'walked east' with a number called steps and a point called here.
  If steps is less than 1 then,
    Return a point, here.
  a point called onward is 'stepped east' of here.
  a number called remaining is steps subtract 1.
  Return a point, 'walked east' of remaining and onward.

(Seven daily distances and the depot they start from: nine argument words in
 all, so the point lands past the six argument registers and is read off the
 stack.)
To 'the last stop after' with a number called monday and a number called tuesday and a number called wednesday and a number called thursday and a number called friday and a number called saturday and a number called sunday and a point called depot.
  Set depot's x to depot's x add monday add tuesday add wednesday add thursday add friday add saturday add sunday.
  Return a point, depot.

To 'the office moved' with a journey called commute.
  Set commute's 'the office''s x to 88.
  Return a journey, commute.

a point called origin.
a point called 'the far corner'.
Set 'the far corner''s x to 100.

(Braces keep each call a separate argument: without them the `and` would be
 read as part of the first call's own argument list.)
a segment called span is 'the segment between' of {'stepped east' of origin} and {'stepped east' of 'the far corner'}.
Print span's start's x.
Print span's end's x.
Print origin's x.

a point called walked is 'walked east' of 5 and origin.
Print walked's x.
Print origin's x.

a point called 'the depot'.
Set 'the depot''s x to 1000.
a point called 'the last stop' is 'the last stop after' of 1 and 2 and 3 and 4 and 5 and 6 and 7 and 'the depot'.
Print 'the last stop''s x.
Print 'the depot''s x.

a journey called commute.
Set commute's home's x to 3.
a journey called 'the new commute' is 'the office moved' of commute.
Print 'the new commute''s 'the office''s x.
Print 'the new commute''s home's x.
Print commute's 'the office''s x.

(A global thing goes into a frame and comes back out. The definition sits
 here, below the declaration it reads: a thing variable is known to the
 parse from where it is declared onwards, so a function above it could not
 name its fields at all.)
To 'shift the origin'.
  a point called shifted is origin.
  Set shifted's x to 7.
  Set origin to shifted.

'shift the origin'.
Print origin's x.
